  "reasoning": "The patient's primary complaints are persistent chest pain and nausea, which are classic hallmarks of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The chronic nature of the pain ('hurting for a while') combined with significant anxiety is highly characteristic of GERD, as patients often experience heightened anxiety due to recurrent chest discomfort that can mimic cardiac issues. While shivering may accompany various conditions, the specific cluster of prolonged chest pain and nausea aligns most closely with acid reflux irritating the esophagus. Other options like peptic ulcer disease typically center on abdominal/stomach pain rather than chest pain, and allergic reactions would prominently feature respiratory or dermatological signs (e.g., throat swelling, breathing difficulty, rashes) which are absent here. Therefore, gastroesophageal reflux disease is the most accurate diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]gastroesophageal reflux disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
